  "bodyText": "Skip to main content\nAlumni & Friends - Ways to get involved and give back\nSite Menu\nSearch\nMain navigation\nHome\nCoping with Academic Stress\nExam Preparation & Test Taking\nMathematics\nNote Taking\nTime Management\nReading\nStudy Skills\nWriting\nResources\nContact\nAbout\nAcorn |\nQuercus |\nUCheck\nFeeling Distressed?\nUniversity of Toronto Scarborough\nUTSC Quick Links\nSearch\nSearch\nq\nGo\nLearning Strategies\nHome\nDigital Vs. Paper Notes\nDigital vs. Paper Notes\n\nWhether to use digital or paper notes is an individual decision that's really based on your learning preferences, disability-related needs, and the content delivered. It's important to make the decision regarding which format to use before the lecture so you've got the necessary resources and equipment in place. You might choose to keep paper notes in a binder or spiral bound notebook, or to create different digital folders on a computer for each course. Here are some benefits of both methods to consider:\n\nBenefits of Digital Notes\n\t\nBenefits of Paper Notes\n\n\nTyping is typically faster than handwriting and may remove barriers resulting from your disability\nEditing notes as you type is easier and more efficient (copy, paste, delete, highlight, bold) \nCan save notes to online folders for access across multiple devices \nEasy to share and compare notes with peers \nCan edit lecture slides, but may be more challenging to format \n\t\nNotebooks can be lighter and easier to carry than laptops \nEasier to write or annotate on top of lecture slides \nEasier to incorporate visual elements and drawing (i.e. doodling to help stay focused) \nHandwriting has been shown to be more effective for memory \n\nDid you know you have free access to Microsoft OneNote\nOpens a new window\n?\n\n \n\nHow to use OneNote | Microsoft\n\nOneNote is a software application that allows you to create and manage digital notebooks, which are collections of notes, drawings, clippings, and other multimedia elements. OneNote is part of the Microsoft Office suite.\nUniversity of Toronto Scarborough\nInspiring Inclusive Excellence\nfacebook\nOpens a new window\n instagram\nOpens a new window\n twitter\nOpens a new window\n youtube\nOpens a new window\n linkedin\nOpens a new window\nIMPORTANT LINKS\nAccessAbility Statement\nCampus Safety\nCampus Status\nCareers@UTSC\nCAMPUS LINKS\nU of T Scarborough Campus\nU of T Home\nU of T St. George (Downtown)\nU of T Mississauga Campus\n\nUniversity of Toronto Scarborough 1265 Military Trail, Toronto, ON. Canada, M1C 1A4\n\nCampus Safety (Non-Emergency) (416) 287-7398\n\nCampus Safety (Emergency) (416) 978-2222\n\nWe wish to acknowledge this land on which the University of Toronto operates. For thousands of years it has been the traditional land of the Wendat Nation, the Seneca, and the Mississaugas of the Credit. Today, this meeting place is still the home to many Indigenous people from across Turtle Island and we are grateful to have the opportunity to work on this land. Statement of Acknowledgement of Traditional Land",
